{
  "title": "Immutable value types",
  "idea": "An `enum` fixes a closed set of named values, a `record` declares an immutable data carrier in one line, and immutable objects never change after construction.",
  "points": [
    "An `enum` turns string states into a closed set; typos stop compiling.",
    "A `record` generates final fields, accessors, `equals`, `hashCode` and `toString`.",
    "Records are only shallowly immutable: copy mutable inputs with `List.copyOf`.",
    "Immutable objects can be shared and cached across threads without locks."
  ],
